# LICENSE-DATA

This file governs ConContexto's DERIVED DATA and EDITORIAL TEXT. It does
NOT cover the Go/Astro source code, which is MIT-licensed — see
`LICENSE`.

## No single licence covers all derived data

There is deliberately no blanket statement here claiming one licence over
every series this project publishes. Each upstream source has its own
terms, and Eurostat's terms are a concrete reason a blanket claim would
be false: Commission Decision 2011/833/EU authorises reuse of Eurostat's
own material with acknowledgement, but that permission does NOT extend
to third-party material Eurostat republishes, and some commercial
redissemination of Eurostat data is separately restricted. Asserting "CC
BY 4.0, no exceptions" over Eurostat-derived series would overclaim.

## What IS authoritative

`config/sources/{source}.yaml` is the authoritative licence record for
every value ConContexto derives from that source: licence name and URL,
required attribution text, access type, and any redistribution
restriction (`licence.redistribution.allowed`,
`licence.redistribution.conditions_md`,
`licence.redistribution.commercial_restrictions_md`). `validate-config`
enforces that every source referenced by a configured series has all of
these fields — see spec `source-attribution-licensing`.

## Default for ConContexto's own editorial text

Where a source's `config/sources/{source}.yaml` permits redistribution
with attribution, ConContexto's own value-added editorial text —
indicator descriptions, and the `rupturas.yaml` / `eventos.yaml` /
`gobiernos.yaml` entries this project authors itself, as distinct from
the underlying source values — is offered under CC BY 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), attributed to
"ConContexto". This default applies to ConContexto's own authored text
only; it does not override any individual source's stricter terms for
the underlying values themselves.

## Before you reuse anything

Read the `config/sources/{source}.yaml` file for the specific source
behind the series you want to reuse. This file explains the mechanism;
it is not a substitute for those per-source terms.
